.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt RM 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm rm
.Nd remove directory entries
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f | i
.Op Fl dIPRrvx
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
removes each
.Ar file Ns 's
name; the file itself goes when no other name or open descriptor keeps it.
A directory is refused unless
.Fl d
or
.Fl R
is given.
Without
.Fl f ,
a file that its user may not write, other than a symbolic link, is asked
about first when the standard input is a terminal.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Remove empty directories too, as
.Xr rmdir 1
does.
.It Fl f
Ask nothing, and say nothing about files that don't exist (nor fail for
them).
Cancels
.Fl i .
.It Fl I
Ask once, before removing anything, if more than three files, or any
directory with
.Fl R ,
are named.
.It Fl i
Ask before removing each file.
Cancels
.Fl f .
.It Fl P
Accepted and ignored, as on
.Fx :
overwriting a file before removing it doesn't reliably destroy its data.
.It Fl R
Remove each directory named and everything under it, depth first.
Symbolic links are removed, never followed.
A directory that isn't removed (because of an answer or an error) keeps
its parents.
.It Fl r
The same as
.Fl R .
.It Fl v
Write the name of each file removed.
.It Fl x
With
.Fl R ,
don't enter directories on another file system than the operand's.
.El
.Pp
Questions are written on the standard error and answered with a line on the
standard input; an answer beginning with
.Ql y
or
.Ql Y
is yes.
.Pp
An operand whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa ..
and one that names the root directory are refused with a diagnostic and left
alone.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
With
.Fl f ,
files that don't exist don't count.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Remove a build tree, saying what goes:
.Dl rm -rv target
.Pp
Remove a file whose name begins with a dash:
.Dl rm -- -file
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rmdir 1 ,
.Xr unlink 1 ,
.Xr rmdir 2 ,
.Xr unlink 2
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2008 ;
.Fl d ,
.Fl I ,
.Fl P ,
.Fl v
and
.Fl x
are extensions, as on the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
